---
/**
 * Keeps the iOS 26 Safari toolbars clear glass, so the page shows from the very top of the screen to
 * the very bottom.
 *
 * Safari tints a toolbar with a solid colour when it finds a "fixed container" at that edge. WebKit's
 * LocalFrameView::fixedContainerEdges hit-tests ONE point per edge: the horizontal centre of the
 * layout viewport, 4px in from the top and the bottom (PageColorSampler::fixedContainerSampleRectMargin).
 * From the element hit there it walks up the ancestors, and the first fixed or sticky box at least 90%
 * as wide as the viewport (stuck or not) becomes the container; its background colour, or sampled
 * pixels, paint the bar. Viewport-sized containers even keep the previous colour, and that state lives
 * on the tab, so one tinted moment (a pinned section passing the edge) sticks across navigations.
 *
 * These two probes sit exactly on those points, above everything. Each is a small, empty, transparent
 * fixed box, which WebKit skips as "hidden or nearly transparent" without looking past it, and it has
 * no fixed or sticky ancestor, so no container is found and no tint is applied, whatever the page has
 * pinned underneath. They must stay hit-testable (pointer-events: auto; never inert, see
 * marketingNavController), but they are 16 × 8px at the screen edges, under Safari's own bars.
 * Static demo pages carry the same markup (public/demo/heritage-grooming).
 */
---

<div class="ks-edge-probe ks-edge-probe--top" aria-hidden="true" data-edge-probe></div>
<div class="ks-edge-probe ks-edge-probe--bottom" aria-hidden="true" data-edge-probe></div>

<style is:global>
  .ks-edge-probe {
    position: fixed;
    left: 50%;
    z-index: 2147483647;
    width: 16px;
    height: 8px;
    margin-left: -8px;
    pointer-events: auto;
  }

  .ks-edge-probe--top {
    top: 0;
  }

  .ks-edge-probe--bottom {
    bottom: 0;
  }

  @media print {
    .ks-edge-probe {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>
